<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belz Voivodeship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> Voivodeship was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, created in 1462 when King Casimir IV took over the Principality of Belz after the local line of the Masovian dukes died out. With its capital at Belz, it lay in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>, in present-day western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> annexed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belz-voivodeship.jpg" alt="Belz Voivodeship" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Poznaniak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RON_wojew%C3%B3dztwo_be%C5%82skie_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a>, on the Zaloka river, was one of the oldest gords, or fortified settlements, of the Czerwien Land. In 981 the area was seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>; Bolesław Chrobry recovered it in 1018, but after his death it passed back to Rus'. In the early 14th century the Land of Belz was inherited by Boleslaw Jerzy II of Mazovia, and after his death on 7 April 1340 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a> together with Belz.</p><p>In 1387 King Wladyslaw Jagiello gave the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> as a dowry to his sister Alexandra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on her marriage to Siemowit IV, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. It stayed with the sons and grandsons of Siemowit IV until the grandsons died childless early in 1462, when it became property of the Crown and was turned into a voivodeship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Together with the Ruthenian Voivodeship, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a> within the Lesser Poland Province, and it was the smallest of the Lesser Poland voivodeships, slightly smaller than the Lublin Voivodeship. It sent four senators to the Senate of the Commonwealth: the Voivode and the Castellan of Bełz and the Castellans of Lubaczów and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Busk</a>. The local sejmik at Belz elected five deputies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, while the general sejmik for all the Ruthenian lands met at Sądowa Wisznia.</p><p>At first the voivodeship was made up of three counties, Lubaczów, Horodło and Szewlock; in 1767 it was divided into the counties of Bełz, Grabowiec, Horodło and Lubaczów and the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Busk</a>. In the 17th century it had 483 villages and 33 towns. Nobles from Mazovia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> and Greater Poland settled in the region: in 1570–1580, among noble families of known origin, 79% were Polish, 15% Ruthenian and 4% Wallachian, while the peasantry was little touched by migration from the west. Its voivodes included Rafał Leszczyński, Jakub Sobieski and Adam Mikołaj Sieniawski.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 almost the whole voivodeship passed to the Habsburg Empire as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. Only the towns of Dubienka and Korytnica, with some villages, remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> in 1793 these were joined to the newly created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a> and Włodzimierz voivodeships. The historian Zygmunt Gloger described the voivodeship in his Historical Geography of the Lands of Old Poland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belz Voivodeship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Masovian line of Belz died out in 1462, King Casimir IV incorporated it into the Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569) as the <b>Belz Voivodeship</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-belz">Principality of Belz</a></li></ul></section>
